<configs>

	<!--
		=========================================================================
		THE ESC MENU BUTTON
		=========================================================================

		Appended after btnSandboxSettings so Help sits with the other "settings and
		information" entries rather than down beside Save and Exit.

		This button is inert on its own. XUiC_InGameMenuWindow.Init looks up exactly ten
		buttons by name and attaches a C# handler to each; an appended button is not in
		that list. Harmony/XUiC_InGameMenuWindow_HelpButton.cs supplies the handler, and
		the name below is the contract between the two - change one, change both.

		insertAfter rather than append: append would put Help below Exit, and the vanilla
		order deliberately keeps the destructive entries last.
	-->
	<insertAfter xpath="/windows/window[@name='ingameMenu']/grid[@name='buttons']/simplebutton[@name='btnSandboxSettings']">
		<simplebutton name="btnSphereIIHelpScreens" depth="3" width="250" height="46"
		              caption_key="helpScreensTitle" font_size="34" />
	</insertAfter>

	<!--
		=========================================================================
		THE HELP SCREEN
		=========================================================================

		Two levels of navigation, both vanilla XUiC_TabSelector:

		    left column (this file)     one button per contributing modlet
		    top tab strip (per section) that modlet's pages

		Nesting is safe - see the note in templates.xml.

		Geometry and chrome are lifted from vanilla's in-game "sandboxOptions" window, so
		it sits and behaves like a first-party screen. The window's own controller,
		XUiC_HelpScreens, exists solely to wire the Close button.

		[ CHILD NAMES THAT MUST NOT CHANGE ]
		XUiC_TabSelector finds its parts by the dotted path in [XuiBindComponent]:
		  tabsHeader    walked into, then searched for TabSelectorButton controllers and
		                for the layout grid
		  tabsContents  walked into, then searched for TabSelectorTab controllers
		Both names repeat at each level - the outer nav has a pair, and so does every
		section. That is fine: each selector's search starts at itself and the outer one
		stops as soon as it matches a section.

		"border" and "backgroundMainTabs" are two more names XUiC_TabSelector looks for -
		optional sprites it re-anchors, found by Single lookups that recurse
		first-match-wins. A stray "border" is unavoidable and harmless: every simplebutton
		contains one, so the selector always binds to some button's. Both of its uses are
		gated on tabHeaderBackground being non-null, so the one that actually matters is
		"backgroundMainTabs" - do not define that, and the border binding is inert.

		[ HOW BUTTONS APPEAR ]
		By INDEX, at both levels. XUiC_TabSelector.Init pairs Tabs[i] with tabButtons[i],
		and each button takes its caption from its partner via {tab_name_localized}.
		Surplus buttons hide themselves (updateTabVisibility: i >= Tabs.Length ->
		IsVisible = false). Both grids use repeat_content="true", so one declaration
		yields rows*cols anonymous buttons and a contributor never declares a button at
		either level.

		[ CAPACITY ]
		Twenty sections (rows="20" on the nav grid), four pages per section.

		The nav column scrolls, so its limit is the rows attribute alone - height stopped
		mattering once the grid moved inside a scrollview. The page strip does NOT scroll,
		so its cols must keep fitting across the 964px section: four 240px tabs.

		Raise either by changing rows/cols and the matching cell size, from this file or
		from a contributor's own file:

		    <set xpath="/windows/window[@name='helpScreens']//rect[@name='nav']/rect[@name='tabsHeader']//grid[@name='tabButtons']/@rows">30</set>

		Never let sections or pages outnumber their buttons: the surplus gets a null
		TabButton, and XUiC_TabSelectorTab.TabSelected dereferences it unguarded. Surplus
		BUTTONS are free - they hide themselves, and because they are always the trailing
		ones, hiding them leaves no gap in the column.

		[ GAMEPAD ]
		The outer nav uses use_page_buttons="true" (Page Up/Down) and sections leave it at
		the default false (Window Paging Left/Right). This is deliberate. Both levels are
		open at once and XUiC_TabSelector.Update is gated only on the view being visible,
		so sharing the binding would step the nav and the page strip together.

		[ WHY A DEFAULT SECTION SHIPS ]
		XUiC_TabSelector logs an error and bails out entirely on zero tabs, so both the
		nav and every section need at least one child. The Getting Started section below
		is that floor, and doubles as the worked example a contributor copies.
	-->
	<append xpath="/windows">

		<window name="helpScreens" anchor="center" pos="-630,400" width="1260" height="800"
		        cursor_area="true" pause_game="true"
		        controller="HelpScreens, SphereIIHelpScreens">

			<rect name="header" height="43" depth="1">
				<headerbg />
				<label style="header.name" pos="6,-6" width="550" text_key="helpScreensTitle" />
			</rect>

			<rect name="content" pos="0,-46" height="${parentinnerheight-46}" depth="1" padding="20">
				<boxbg />

				<rect name="nav" depth="2" width="1220" height="655" controller="TabSelector"
				      use_page_buttons="true"
				      select_tab_contents_on_open="false" select_tab_contents_on_change="false">

					<!--
						Left column of modlet buttons, scrolling.

						Only 13 buttons fit in the 655px column at 48px each, and XUi rects do
						not clip, so beyond that the surplus used to draw over the Close button
						and off the panel. The grid now sits inside a scrollview, so the column
						is bounded by the grid's rows attribute rather than by its height.

						The scroll rig is the same one helpscreen_page uses, and the same one
						vanilla's sandboxOptions window uses: a plain rect carrying on_scroll,
						holding a defaultscrollbar and a scrollview as siblings.
						XUiV_ScrollView.InitView finds its bar with
						Parent.TryGetChildView<XUiV_ScrollBar>(""), so the two must share a
						parent - hence navScrollArea rather than hanging the bar off tabsHeader.

						"tabsHeader" stays the name of the outer rect because XUiC_TabSelector
						binds through it. Nesting the buttons deeper is safe: that lookup is an
						array lookup, which recurses until it matches, so the TabSelectorButtons
						are still found inside the scrollview.

						CHROME: the boxbg/boxcaption pair is vanilla's titled sub-panel idiom -
						border only, no fill, inset 1px, with a 14px gap along the top for the
						caption to sit in. It appears 20 times across the shipped in-game
						windows, always in exactly this form.

						Widths: navScrollArea 240, defaultscrollbar takes the right 16
						(it positions itself at ${parentinnerwidth-16}), leaving 220 for the
						scrollview and 216 for the button inside it.

						Vertically the scroll area starts at -24 and the grid inside it is inset
						a further 10 (see below), so the first button still lands at -34, clear
						of the caption at roughly -1 to -31. The 10px the grid gives up is the
						clip softness band, which is empty at rest - putting it above the caption
						line rather than below keeps the button where it looks right without
						reintroducing the fade.
					-->
					<rect name="tabsHeader" pos="0,0" width="250" height="655" depth="0">
						<boxbg borderleft="1" borderright="1" bordertop="14" borderbottom="1" backsprite="" />
						<boxcaption text_key="helpNavCaption" />

						<rect name="navScrollArea" pos="5,-24" width="240" height="616" depth="1"
						      on_scroll="true" gamepad_selectable="true" snap="false" use_selection_box="true">

							<defaultscrollbar />

							<!--
								The grid is inset by the clipping softness, not parked at 0,0.

								clippingsoftness maps to NGUI's panel.clipSoftness, which fades
								anything within that many pixels of the clip edge - by distance,
								not by scroll position. Content flush against the top of the
								viewport is therefore faded permanently, which read as the first
								button being blurry along its top edge on open.

								Starting the grid 10px down puts the first button clear of the
								band, so it is crisp at rest, while content that scrolls up past
								the edge still fades out the way it should.
							-->
							<scrollview pos="0,0" width="220" clippingsoftness="0,10">
								<grid name="tabButtons" pos="0,-10" depth="2" rows="20" cols="1"
								      cell_width="220" cell_height="48" repeat_content="true"
								      arrangement="vertical">
									<helpscreen_navbutton />
								</grid>
							</scrollview>
						</rect>
					</rect>

					<!--
						One section per contributing modlet. A contributor appends a sibling of
						the block below with:

						  <append xpath="/windows/window[@name='helpScreens']//rect[@name='nav']/rect[@name='tabsContents']">

						Note the xpath is anchored on rect[@name='nav'] - "tabsContents" alone
						also matches the one inside every section.

						CHROME: both sprites below live here rather than inside each section, so
						every contributor gets the same framing without repeating it - and so a
						section written before this existed still looks right. boxbg fills and
						frames the whole right panel; pageFrame rings the page area beneath the
						tab strip, which starts at y -40 in every section.
					-->
					<rect name="tabsContents" depth="2" pos="256,0" width="964" height="655">

						<boxbg />

						<sprite depth="9" name="pageFrame" pos="0,-38" width="964" height="617"
						        sprite="menu_empty2px" type="sliced" fillcenter="false" color="[black]" />

						<rect name="sectionGettingStarted" controller="TabSelectorTab" tab_key="helpSectionGettingStarted">
							<rect name="tabs" width="964" height="655" controller="TabSelector"
							      select_tab_contents_on_open="false" select_tab_contents_on_change="false">

								<rect name="tabsHeader" height="38" depth="0">
									<grid name="tabButtons" pos="3,-2" depth="2" rows="1" cols="4"
									      cell_width="240" cell_height="36" repeat_content="true"
									      arrangement="horizontal">
										<helpscreen_pagebutton />
									</grid>
								</rect>

								<rect name="tabsContents" depth="2" pos="0,-40" width="964" height="615">
									<sprite depth="0" name="pageBackground" sprite="menu_empty3px"
									        color="[mediumGrey]" type="sliced" fillcenter="true" />

									<helpscreen_page tab_key="helpPageAbout"   text_key="helpPageAboutBody" />
									<helpscreen_page tab_key="helpPageModders" text_key="helpPageModdersBody" />
								</rect>
							</rect>
						</rect>

					</rect>
				</rect>

				<!--
					Close button. Bottom right of the 1220x714 padded content area, below the
					nav: 1220-200 across, 714-46 down.

					Deliberately a sibling of the nav rect, not a child. It belongs to the
					window as a whole and must stay put and clickable whichever section and
					page are showing. XUiC_HelpScreens.Init finds it by name and hooks
					OnPressed; ESC also closes the window, but returns straight to the game
					rather than to the pause menu.
				-->
				<simplebutton name="btnClose" depth="3" pos="1020,-668" width="200" height="46"
				              font_size="34" caption_key="xuiClose" />
			</rect>
		</window>

	</append>

</configs>
